{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_threshold",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "threshold_surface",
      "parent_relation": "The steady activation curve is a bounded monotone function of voltage alone, centered by one fixed V_half with one shared voltage offset for both sequential permissive steps.",
      "child_relation": "Retain the sequential-occupancy structure but allow the effective half-activation position to shift with an additional sampled ionic-condition input, so the voltage-to-activation map becomes condition-dependent rather than sharing one universal threshold.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "V"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "Across fixed ionic conditions, depolarization still increases activation monotonically, but the whole activation curve is measurably left- or right-shifted between conditions; one parent parameter set with a single V_half cannot match both the low-voltage onset and midrange occupancy across those conditions simultaneously."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "eta",
        "expression": "(V - V_half)/slope_factor",
        "role": "parent reduced voltage coordinate controlling the present sigmoidal occupancy"
      },
      {
        "name": "exp_eta",
        "expression": "exp((V - V_half)/slope_factor)",
        "role": "single-step permissive bias entering the sequential partition function"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "The added effect is observable when recordings are compared across at least two admissible fixed ionic-condition settings that alter electrostatic stabilization of permissive states while temperature remains fixed; the shift must be comparable to a non-negligible fraction of slope_factor over the sampled activation range.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, the mechanism appears as consistent threshold shifts between a subset of ionic-condition settings; in holdout, the same condition-dependent shift persists at other settings or voltages, extending the same monotone family rather than introducing a separate branch.",
    "continuation_path": "The parent law is recovered when the new ionic-condition dependence vanishes, so the threshold shift is zero and V_half is effectively constant across all sampled conditions.",
    "freedom_change": "add_input",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "With only voltage supplied, any ionic-condition-induced threshold displacement is hidden inside one fixed fitted V_half. A new independently sampled condition input is necessary to express a genuine change in the scored static mapping rather than just refitting one parameter per dataset.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes a universal activation threshold despite the experiment class allowing comparisons across fixed but different ionic environments that can shift steady activation without introducing kinetics.",
    "baseline_assumption": "V_half is treated as a constant parameter independent of ionic conditions.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Changes in ionic conditions can alter surface-charge screening or state stabilization, producing a condition-dependent shift of the voltage range over which permissive conformations become occupied at steady state.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "V",
      "V_half",
      "slope_factor"
    ],
    "rationale": "Parent response relationship: at fixed temperature and fixed ionic conditions, m_inf rises sigmoidally with V using one shared threshold parameter V_half and one shared slope_factor, so all admissible curves are the same shape and center after refitting a single parameter set. Proposed refinement: release the idealization that the activation center is universal by introducing one sampled ionic-condition input that shifts the effective threshold while preserving bounded steady occupancy and monotone depolarization dependence. Varied condition: ionic environment; held fixed within each curve: temperature and the chosen ionic setting; varied within each curve: clamped voltage. Observable contrast: between two ionic settings, the child predicts a reproducible horizontal displacement of the activation curve, so the voltage at a given occupancy differs across conditions. A single parent parameter set cannot reproduce this because it has only one fixed threshold for all supplied observations; matching one condition's onset and midpoint forces the same center on the other condition. This is a changed target mapping, not merely an auxiliary reinterpretation, because the scored output m_inf becomes a function of both voltage and ionic condition. The refinement remains scientifically admissible when the condition-induced shift is moderate, preserving boundedness, monotonicity, and finite asymptotes; if ionic conditions are truly held to one fixed value for the entire dataset, then no observable complexity gain would be defensible."
  },
